WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.480
Strip away the chemistry and the citric acid cycle has one real job.

2
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.680
Harvest high energy electrons from carbon fuel.

3
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.140
A two-carbon acetyl fragment enters and condenses onto a four-carbon receiver to

4
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:21.760
make six carbons. Around the ring, we lose two carbons as carbon dioxide and shrink

5
00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:29.340
back down, and as we do, we capture electrons in NADH and FADH₂ and squeeze out a

6
00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:35.820
single ATP. Notice the carbon bookkeeping along the rim, six to five to four, and

7
00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:39.960
notice how every step that drops a carbon also banks reducing power.

8
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:45.220
The cycle is not really about making ATP directly, it is an electron harvesting

9
00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:46.980
machine feeding the next stage.

10
00:00:47.833 --> 00:00:50.033
Think of the cycle as a two-stage machine.

11
00:00:50.653 --> 00:00:56.233
In stage one, oxidation, two carbons are introduced as an acetyl group onto

12
00:00:56.233 --> 00:01:02.673
oxaloacetate to form citrate and that six-carbon citrate undergoes two oxidative

13
00:01:02.673 --> 00:01:07.393
decarboxylations, burning off two molecules of carbon dioxide.

14
00:01:07.873 --> 00:01:09.553
That is the burn carbon half.

15
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:13.733
Stage two is regeneration, rebuilding the catalyst.

16
00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:20.433
The remaining backbone is reworked step by step until oxaloacetate is restored and

17
00:01:20.433 --> 00:01:22.713
ready to accept the next acetyl group.

18
00:01:23.293 --> 00:01:26.093
The dividing line in the middle is the stage boundary.

19
00:01:26.793 --> 00:01:31.473
Both halves matter because both generate the high-energy electrons that ultimately

20
00:01:31.473 --> 00:01:35.793
power ATP synthesis in oxidative phosphorylation downstream.

21
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:39.277
The regulation of the cycle is not arbitrary.

22
00:01:39.837 --> 00:01:42.177
It tracks the cell's energy balance directly.

23
00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:48.657
In a high-energy state, when ATP and NADH are abundant, the cell does not need to

24
00:01:48.657 --> 00:01:53.817
burn more fuel, so these molecules act as signals that slow the cycle down at its

25
00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:55.617
committed, irreversible steps.

26
00:01:56.237 --> 00:02:02.857
In a low-energy state, when ADP piles up and ATP is scarce, the brakes come off and

27
00:02:02.857 --> 00:02:05.117
the cycle speeds up to regenerate ATP.

28
00:02:05.937 --> 00:02:11.117
The same logic coordinates pyruvate dehydrogenase upstream, so feeding into the

29
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:13.357
cycle and running the cycle stay matched.

30
00:02:13.977 --> 00:02:18.337
Supply chases demand, and the controls sit exactly where flux commits.

31
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.050
Here is a puzzle worth sitting with.

32
00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:26.710
Oxygen never appears anywhere in the citric acid cycle, yet the cycle is strictly

33
00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:34.530
aerobic. Why? The cycle produces NADH and FADH₂, but it can only keep running if it

34
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.910
gets NAD⁺ plus and FAD back.

35
00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:42.350
Those carriers are regenerated only when the electron transport chain re-oxidizes

36
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:47.150
them, and the electron transport chain hands its electrons to oxygen at the very

37
00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:53.110
end. Remove oxygen and the chain backs up, the carriers stay reduced, NAD⁺ plus and

38
00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:56.350
FAD never return, and the cycle grinds to a halt.

39
00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:01.230
So the cycle depends on oxygen indirectly, through the carriers it must recycle.

40
00:03:02.083 --> 00:03:06.563
Think of the cell as financially literate, constantly converting between currencies.

41
00:03:07.123 --> 00:03:11.863
The citric acid cycle does not finish the job, it hands off the charge.

42
00:03:12.363 --> 00:03:19.143
Most of its output is not cash-in-hand ATP but reducing power, NADH and FADH₂,

43
00:03:19.523 --> 00:03:22.303
which is like a high-value asset that has to be sold.

44
00:03:22.783 --> 00:03:28.083
The electron transport chain is where that sale happens, converting each NADH into

45
00:03:28.083 --> 00:03:35.203
roughly 2.5 ATP and each FADH₂ into about 1.5 by reducing oxygen.

46
00:03:35.743 --> 00:03:40.023
So the cycle banks the electrons and the next pathway liquidates them.

47
00:03:40.643 --> 00:03:44.843
Keep that hand off in mind, the real ATP windfall comes downstream.
